+Label values from the three selected Amazon galleries; portion sizes differ. Anthony’s original structured listing field says 30 servings, whereas both its back-pouch image and nutrition closeup say 56. The table preserves the label reading rather than reconciling the discrepancy.[1][2][3]
+
+**Sprout Living: a protein serving with a simple-ingredient story.** Its 20 g protein declaration belongs to a 32.5 g serving. The front says “One Ingredient” and “Single-Source Protein Powder,” and the ingredient artwork lists organic pressed pumpkin seed powder. That conflicts with the original listing’s structured “Protein Source: Blend” field. The gallery therefore strengthens the evidence for the seller’s single-ingredient positioning; it does not establish the physical composition of a purchased batch. The ingredient line says “Pressed,” while the listing bullets supply the more specific cold-pressed claim.[1]
+
+**Anthony’s: a small recipe addition with a documented processing description.** The 5 g protein figure belongs to an 8 g tablespoon, not to the larger portions used by the other two brands. Its back pouch describes toasting the seeds, pressing out oil and finely milling the remaining pulp. The front also says “COLD-PRESSED.” Toasting and pressing describe different stages, so their coexistence is not automatically a contradiction; it does mean that cold-pressed should not be read as proof of an entirely unheated product. The actual discrepancy is the serving count: 30 in the original structured field versus 56 in the gallery. Neither a variant change nor a reformulation is established.[2]
+
+**Micro Ingredients: a larger pouch for varied daily uses.** Its 2 lb (907 g) front states 18 g protein per serving; the back specifies two scoops, approximately 30 g. The directions separately call for “2 rounded scoops” in smoothies, desserts, juice, oatmeal or yogurt. Alongside the original listing’s coffee and baking suggestions, this creates an everyday-ingredient proposition rather than a sports-only one. Fine-milled and raw remain seller descriptions, not measurements of particle size or processing temperature.[3]
+
+The label comparison explains portion conventions, not a purity hierarchy. Comparing 5 g, 18 g and 20 g without their serving masses would obscure the central difference. Converting rounded declarations into purported batch protein percentages would add precision the evidence does not supply.
 
 For a new product, the opportunity suggested by these examples is to make one use occasion convincing rather than attach every possible use to the same powder. A kitchen ingredient can invite blending and baking without promising instant dissolution. A shake mix needs an explicit preparation method. A coffee addition creates a different sensory expectation again. The sample identifies those possible briefs; it does not tell us which would sell best.
 
